  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...There has been just enough moisture for a few \nshowers to develop, mainly along and inland of the Treasure Coast \nwith the inland moving east coast sea breeze. Hi-res guidance \nindicating some additional isolated shower development is possible \nfarther north across the interior as boundary shifts inland this \nafternoon and early evening, so have added a slight chance for \nshowers across these areas. Otherwise, much of the region will \nremain mostly dry and any rainfall that does occur with any stray \nshowers should be light (around a tenth of an inch or less). Low \nlevel onshore flow will continue to weaken into tonight, and there \nexists a better potential (~40-50% chance) for patchy fog \ndevelopment late tonight into early Friday morning. Any fog that \nforms will be capable of producing localized visibilities of a mile \nor less, with fog gradually lifting and dissipating soon after \ndaybreak Friday.  \n  \nFriday...Weak front across the southeast U.S. will continue to push \nslowly southeastward on Friday. Ahead of the front moisture \nincreases ever so slightly, and into the afternoon isolated showers \nwill again be possible near to north of Orlando. Additional \ndevelopment of isolated showers is then forecast farther south \nacross the interior and toward the Volusia/Brevard County coast into \nthe evening. However, not expecting much in the way of rainfall \naccumulations with this activity. For now have kept rain chances \naround 20 percent. Highs will remain warmer than normal in the \nmid 80s, and overnight lows will drop to the mid to upper 60s. \n\nSaturday-Wednesday...(Modified Previous Discussion) Weak cool front \nwill push southward through central Florida Saturday. Limited \nmoisture with this boundary means it will likely be a dry frontal \npassage. A wind shift out of the N/NW will announce its passage. \nWinds gradually veer N/NE Sun and NE Mon as high pressure builds in \nbehind this boundary. GFS and ECMWF are in better agreement showing \na weaker mid-level S/W trough moving across the deep South, but \ntiming differences remain on when it crosses Florida into next week. \nThe GFS is the faster of the two, increasing rainfall across the \narea Wednesday with the ECMWF holding off better rainfall potential \nuntil Wednesday night/Thursday. Rain chances for the time being \nremain out of the forecast this weekend, with only low rain chances \n(10-20%) for much of the region through the early to middle \nportion of next week. Given the weaker upper support, severe \nweather looks unlikely and currently do not have thunder \nmentioned. Temperatures trend cooler this weekend behind the front\nthough Sat still looks warm in the low to mid 80s. Near normal \nhighs in the upper 70s/lower 80s Sun-Tue. Min temps forecast in \nthe upper 50s NW of I-4 early next week with 60s elsewhere.",
